### Description

This Terraform program defines an Application Load Balancer (ALB) in AWS. The ALB is configured with basic settings and includes nested blocks for access logs and subnet mappings. The program assumes that the VPC and subnets are already created and available. The ALB is set to be internet-facing and uses the default HTTP listener.

### Terraform HCL Program

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

resource "aws_lb" "my_application_load_balancer" {
  name               = "my-application-load-balancer"
  internal           = false
  load_balancer_type = "application"

  subnet_mapping {
    subnet_id = "subnet-0123456789abcdef0"  # Replace with your subnet ID
  }

  access_logs {
    bucket = "my-alb-access-logs-bucket"  # Replace with your S3 bucket name
    prefix = "alb-logs/"
  }

  timeouts {
    create = "10m"
    update = "10m"
    delete = "10m"
  }
}
```

### Notes

- **Provider Configuration**: The AWS provider is configured to use the `us-west-2` region. You can change this to any other valid AWS region.
- **Subnet Mapping**: The `subnet_mapping` block requires a valid subnet ID. Replace `"subnet-0123456789abcdef0"` with your actual subnet ID.
- **Access Logs**: The `access_logs` block requires an S3 bucket name where the logs will be stored. Replace `"my-alb-access-logs-bucket"` with your actual S3 bucket name.
- **Timeouts**: The `timeouts` block specifies the time durations for creating, updating, and deleting the load balancer. These values are set to 10 minutes each, but you can adjust them as needed.

This configuration ensures that the Application Load Balancer is properly set up with the necessary settings and dependencies.

